      "sequence_position": 1,
      "problem": "Calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}$ for $f(x)=5\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3-2x)+7.$",
      "worked_solution": "First, we express $f(x)=5\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3-2x)+7$ as the composite function\n\n$$\nf=5\\operatorname{ln}⁡u+7,\\,u=3-2x.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}=\\frac{5}{u},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-2.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{u}\\cdot (-2) \\\\  & =-\\frac{10}{u} \\\\  & =-\\frac{10}{3-2x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
